@end menu

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

md-mode needs Emacs 29.1 or later and nothing else.  No pandoc, no Node, no
browser: rendering goes through @code{shr}, the HTML engine that ships with
Emacs and drives @code{eww}.

Clone the repository and put it on your @code{load-path}:

@example
git clone https://github.com/hayate/md-mode.git ~/.emacs.d/md-mode
@end example

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/.emacs.d/md-mode")
(autoload 'md-mode "md-mode" "Render the Markdown in this buffer." t)
@end example

With @code{use-package} on a recent Emacs:

@example
(use-package md-mode
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/hayate/md-mode" :rev :newest)
  :commands (md-mode md-split))
@end example

There is nothing to compile and no build step.  @code{md-mode} is autoloaded,
so nothing is read until you first run it.

@node Reading
@chapter Reading a document

@deffn Command md-mode
Render the Markdown in the current buffer, or return to the source.

In a Markdown buffer, replace the window with the rendered document.  In a
rendered document, switch back to the Markdown it came from.  Point is carried
across in both directions, so you return to the passage you were reading.
@end deffn

The rendered document lives in its own read-only buffer.  The file on disk is
never touched by rendering, and cannot be saved in rendered form.

@section Keys

@table @kbd
@item q
Back to the Markdown source.
@item g
Re-render.
@item s
Source and rendered document side by side, in step.  @xref{Side by side}.
@item @key{RET}
Follow the link at point.  @xref{Navigating}.
@item @key{TAB}
Fold or unfold the section at point.  Anywhere else, move to the next link.
@item S-@key{TAB}
Cycle the folding of the whole document.
@item n
@itemx p
Next and previous link.
@item i
Jump to a heading with @code{imenu}.
@item t
Jump to a heading chosen from a table of contents.
@item l
@itemx r
Back and forward through documents you have followed.
@item ^
Open the directory the document lives in.
@end table

@section When it re-renders

The view re-renders when you save the source, when you revert it, and when the
window width changes.  Folded sections stay folded across all of these.

A rendered buffer holds one layout, so it cannot serve two windows of
different widths at once.  Showing a view in a second window leaves it alone;
press @kbd{g} in the window you want it sized for.

@node Navigating
@chapter Navigating

A design document is usually half prose and half an index into a codebase.
md-mode makes that index navigable.

@menu
* File references::     Opening the code a document talks about.
* Other documents::     Walking a folder of specs.
* Outline::             Folding and jumping in a long document.
* Side by side::        Source and document in step.
@end menu

@node File references
@section File references

An inline code span holding something like @code{lib/thing.py:147} becomes a
link that opens the file at line 147.

It becomes a link @emph{only if the file is actually there}.  An unreachable
reference stays ordinary inline code.  That makes linkiness a live signal that
the document and the code still agree: move the document, or the file, and the
link quietly stops being one.

A reference is resolved against, in order:

@enumerate
@item
the document's own directory;
@item
the project root of that directory, found with @code{project-current} or by
looking for a @file{.git};
@item
each entry of @code{md-link-roots}.
@end enumerate

Set @code{md-link-roots} when a document points into a different checkout than
the one it lives in, which is the normal case for a folder of specs:

@example
;; .dir-locals.el in your specs directory
((nil . ((md-link-roots . ("~/src/api" "~/src/models")))))
@end example

@subsection What counts as a reference

The grammar is matched against a whole code span, never searched for inside
one.  Searching substrings finds references that were never written: the
@code{total} of @code{@{"total": 42@}}, or the @code{12} of a timestamp like
@code{12:30}.

A candidate must have no whitespace, must not carry a URL scheme or a Windows
drive letter, and must look like a path -- that is, contain a directory
separator or end in a file extension.  A bare line number such as
@code{:513} is @emph{not} resolved against a path mentioned earlier: the order
in which a renderer walks a paragraph is not the order in which its author was
thinking, and a confidently wrong link is worse than none.

@deffn Command md-link-follow
Follow the link at point.  A file reference opens the file at its line.  A
link to a local Markdown document opens it rendered.  Other local files open
normally, and anything with a scheme goes to the browser.
@end deffn

@node Other documents
@section Other documents

A link to a local Markdown file opens it rendered rather than in a browser, so
a folder of cross-referencing specs reads like a small wiki.  Each document
keeps its own view.

@deffn Command md-link-back
Return to the document visited before this one.
@end deffn

@deffn Command md-link-forward
Undo a @code{md-link-back}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command md-link-up
Open the directory the document lives in.
@end deffn

A link to a local file that does not exist is reported rather than handed to
the browser, which would otherwise try to fetch @samp{./missing.md} as a URL.

@node Outline
@section Outline

Headings in a rendered document are real outline headings, even though the
buffer contains no @code{#} markup: @code{shr} records the level of each
heading as a text property, and Emacs 29 added @code{outline-search-function},
which can search for exactly that.

@kbd{TAB} folds and unfolds a section, @kbd{S-@key{TAB}} cycles the whole
document, and @code{imenu} works.

@deffn Command md-outline-toc
Jump to a heading chosen from the document's table of contents, indented by
level.
@end deffn

Folds are overlays, and a re-render erases the buffer they live in.  Folded
sections are therefore remembered by heading text and folded again afterwards,
so resizing the window does not unfold everything you had put away.

@node Side by side
@section Side by side

@deffn Command md-split
Show the Markdown source and the rendered document in two windows that follow
each other.  Call it again to put the windows back.
@end deffn

The two sides are kept in step by @emph{source line}, not by buffer position.
This matters.  Rendering is lossy: a whole paragraph maps to the line it
started on, so mapping a position across and back does not return where it
began.  Round-tripping raw positions would make point crawl backwards a little
on every command.  An anchor moves only when you move it, and both sides are
set to the same value, so there is nothing to drift.

@node Options
@chapter Options

@section Rendering

@defopt md-render-max-width
Maximum width, in characters, of a rendered line.  Default 100.  Long lines
are hard to read, so the text is capped even in a wide window.  Values below
20 are treated as 20.
@end defopt

@defopt md-render-remote-images
Whether to download and display images with remote URLs.  Default @code{nil}.
@xref{Safety}.
@end defopt

@defopt md-render-highlight-code
Whether to syntax-highlight fenced code blocks.  Default @code{t}.
@end defopt

@defopt md-render-language-modes
Map from a fenced-code language name to the major mode that highlights it.
An allowlist; @pxref{Safety}.
@end defopt

@defopt md-render-max-highlight-size
Fenced code blocks larger than this many characters are not highlighted.
Default 20000.
@end defopt

@defopt md-render-max-image-size
Image files larger than this many bytes are not displayed.  Default 8388608.
@end defopt

@section Parsing

@defopt md-parse-front-matter
Whether to render YAML front matter instead of hiding it.  Default
@code{nil}, which is what GitHub does.
@end defopt

@defopt md-parse-html-blocks
Whether to parse raw HTML blocks and splice them into the document.  Default
@code{t}.
@end defopt

@section The view

@defopt md-view-margin
Columns of blank left margin.  Default 2; @code{0} disables it.
@end defopt

@defopt md-view-hide-line-numbers
Whether to hide line numbers in a rendered document.  Default @code{t}.  A
rendered buffer's line numbers count rendered lines, which correspond to
nothing the reader can use.
@end defopt

@defopt md-auto-rerender-max-size
Documents larger than this many characters are not re-rendered
automatically.  Default 200000.  They still re-render with @kbd{g}.
@end defopt

@defopt md-rerender-delay
Seconds of idle time after a window resize before re-rendering.  Default 0.3.
@end defopt

@section Navigation

@defopt md-link-roots
Extra directories that file references may resolve inside.
@xref{File references}.
@end defopt

@defopt md-link-max-references
Most file references resolved in one document.  Default 500.
@end defopt

@defopt md-link-follow-markdown
Whether a link to a local Markdown file opens rendered.  Default @code{t}.
@end defopt

@defopt md-outline-fold-on-render
Whether folded sections stay folded when the document is re-rendered.
Default @code{t}.
@end defopt

@section Faces

@code{md-code-block}, @code{md-blockquote} and @code{md-blockquote-bar}, plus
the standard @code{shr-h1} through @code{shr-h6}, @code{shr-link} and
@code{shr-text}.

@node Safety
@chapter Safety

Markdown files arrive in cloned repositories, so a document is treated as
untrusted input.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Remote images are not fetched.  They render as a label.  Opening a document
should not make a network request to whoever wrote it.

@item
URLs go through a scheme allowlist.  Anything else, @samp{javascript:} and
@samp{data:} included, is dropped, and the label is left inert.  Control
characters are stripped before the scheme is read, so an entity-encoded
newline cannot smuggle one past.  This applies to Markdown links as well as to
raw HTML.

@item
A file reference must canonicalise to a path inside one of its allowed roots,
so a document cannot make a link out of @samp{../../../../etc/passwd}.

@item
Remote names such as @samp{/ssh:host:/x.png} are refused before any filesystem
call, and before project discovery.  Merely asking whether such a file is
readable opens a connection.

@item
Raw HTML goes through a tag allowlist.  @code{script}, @code{style},
@code{base}, @code{object}, @code{iframe}, @code{svg}, form and media tags are
dropped before anything reaches the renderer.

@item
A fenced code block's language selects a mode from
@code{md-render-language-modes}, an allowlist.  A mode name is never derived
from a fence label, so a document cannot activate an arbitrary major mode and
run its body.

@item
Nesting depth, both block and inline, HTML node count, reference count and
image size are all bounded.  The inline scanner precomputes delimiter matches
and remembers failed searches, so a document full of unmatched brackets cannot
freeze Emacs.
@end itemize

Local image paths are @emph{not} confined to the document's directory.  A
document may point at @file{../assets/logo.png}, which real repositories do
constantly.  Nothing leaves the machine, so the containment would cost more
than it buys.

@node Design
@chapter Design

@example
markdown text --md-parse--> DOM --shr--> rendered buffer
@end example

@table @file
@item md-parse.el
Markdown to DOM.  No UI dependencies; the tested unit.
@item md-render.el
DOM to buffer, via @code{shr}.
@item md-link.el
File references and links out of the document.
@item md-outline.el
Folding, imenu, table of contents.
@item md-mode.el
The command, the view mode, the split view and history.
@end table

@code{md-parse.el} produces the DOM representation @code{dom.el} uses and
@code{shr} consumes: @code{(TAG ATTRIBUTE-ALIST CHILD@dots{})}, with strings
as text nodes.  No HTML text is generated anywhere, so there is nothing to
escape and nothing to reparse.

@code{md-render.el} hands that tree to @code{shr} and overrides only the tags
whose defaults are wrong for a Markdown reader: @code{pre} gains syntax
highlighting, @code{blockquote} gains its bar, @code{img} resolves relative
paths and refuses the network, and lists lose the blank line @code{shr} puts
around a nested one.  Every other block tag is wrapped only to record its
source line.

@section Rendering is a transaction

A render erases and rebuilds the buffer.  Everything anchored to a position in
it -- point, window starts, folds, caches, imenu markers -- would therefore be
destroyed.

Rendering is consequently a transaction.  Before the buffer is rebuilt, that
state is captured as @emph{source lines and heading names}, which survive
because they describe the document rather than the text that happens to be
displaying it.  Afterwards the state is resolved against the new buffer and
caches that pointed into the old one are dropped.

Anything added to md-mode later should do the same: hold a source line, not a
position.

